s=open('script.py').read()
def sub(old_start, new):
    global s
    i=s.index(old_start); j=s.index('\n', i)
    s=s[:i]+new+s[j:]
sub(' "Picture ten thousand', ' "Picture ten thousand processes running one simulation. At some instant, every one of them finishes a timestep and says: I need to save my piece of the answer. Right now.",')
sub(' "One disk can\'t take that.', ' "One disk can\'t take that. One server can\'t either. A single box has a ceiling, and ten thousand writers hit it immediately.",')
sub(' "In this video we\'ll look', ' "In this video we\'ll look at one of the oldest, most widely deployed answers: G P F S, the General Parallel File System, which IBM now calls Storage Scale. A little history first, then most of our time on how it works, and finally what real systems look like.",')
sub(' "A file written this way', ' "A file written this way isn\'t on one disk; it\'s on all of them. Read it, and every disk works at once, so bandwidth adds up. The paper is explicit that this happens inside the file system itself, across several thousand disks in the largest installations of the day.",')
sub(' "The block size matters', ' "Block size matters enormously. Disks are great at streaming and terrible at repositioning. If every request pays a fixed positioning cost, small blocks spend their time moving, and big blocks spend it transferring. In the two thousand two paper, a typical block was two hundred fifty six kilobytes, configurable up to a megabyte. On Oak Ridge\'s Summit, it\'s sixteen megabytes.",')
sub(' "There\'s a catch, though.', ' "There\'s a catch: most files are small. If each consumed a whole sixteen megabyte block, you\'d waste nearly everything. So blocks are divided into subblocks, as small as one thirty second of a block in the paper, and small files and file tails get only the fragments they need.",')
sub(' "Now, who does what?', ' "Now, who does what? In the purest shared-disk setup, every node sees every disk through a storage network. That works, but it gets impractical with thousands of compute nodes.",')
sub(' "So big systems add a layer.', ' "So big systems add a layer. Disks are exposed as N S D\'s, network shared disks. A smaller set of N S D servers attach to the storage, and everyone else reaches the disks across the network, through them. To a client it still looks like one shared pool, which is why the paper calls the design shared-disk.",')
sub(' "Importantly, the N S D servers', ' "The N S D servers are not the brains, though. They move blocks. The intelligence is spread across ordinary nodes in assigned roles. A few are quorum nodes, and one of those is the cluster manager: it hands out leases, detects failures, and runs recovery.",')
sub(' "Each file system also gets', ' "Each file system also gets a file system manager, for disk configuration, space allocation and quotas. Then there are token managers, several of them, each owning a slice of the lock state, divided up by inode number.",')
sub(' "Apart from the quorum role', ' "Apart from the quorum role, every node is identical, and roles can move. If the cluster manager dies, the quorum nodes elect another. To avoid a split brain, only the group holding a majority of nodes may keep using the file system. The minority is fenced off from the disks.",')
sub(' "Here\'s the problem everything', ' "Here\'s the problem everything else is built around. Posix promises that if one process writes and another then reads, the reader sees the write, even across machines. G P F S keeps that promise across the whole cluster; the paper\'s phrase is single-node-equivalent semantics. How, without asking a central server for permission on every operation?",')
sub(' "The answer is tokens.', ' "The answer is tokens. A token is a permission slip: this node may cache and operate on this object, in this way. The token manager hands them out, and each node\'s local lock manager makes fine-grained decisions with no network round trip, for as long as the node holds the right token.",')
sub(' "The inode has a similar', ' "The inode has a similar problem. Every writer changes the file\'s size and modification time, and if each updated the inode on disk, it would be a stampede. So G P F S elects a metanode for each open file. Writers send inode changes to it, and it alone reads and writes that inode on disk. For truly fine-grained sharing, the paper also describes data shipping, where each block has an owner and everyone else sends their I O to it.",')
sub(' "You can see all of this', ' "You can see all of this in a benchmark. On Summit\'s file system, the I O five hundred test, with five hundred and four nodes, has an easy mode, where every process writes its own file, and a hard mode, where all of them write small, unaligned, interleaved chunks into one shared file. Easy: about two terabytes per second. Hard: about half a gigabyte per second. A factor of roughly thirty eight hundred, on identical hardware.",')
sub(' "So far we\'ve talked', ' "So far, nodes and locks. What about the disks? At scale, disks fail constantly. In a traditional RAID group, a rebuild hammers the few disks in that group, and every rebuilt byte lands on one spare. With today\'s drive sizes that takes a long time, and the array is exposed the whole way.",')
sub(' "To build intuition', ' "To build intuition, I ran a toy simulation: same disks, one failure, eight plus two coding. A rebuild can go only as fast as its busiest disk. With sixty disks, declustering cuts that load about six-fold, and the gain grows with the disk count. That\'s bigger than IBM\'s three to four times, as expected, since real systems also have networks, processors, and live application traffic. Trust the documentation\'s number; use the model for the shape of the idea.",')
sub(' "And because silent corruption', ' "And because silent corruption is real, the client appends an eight byte checksum to data before it leaves, and the server verifies it, and checks again on read. Flaky disks aren\'t kicked out immediately: a disk hospital times out an operation after about ten seconds, then diagnoses whether the disk or its path is at fault, while redundancy keeps serving reads.",')
sub(' "That software runs on', ' "All of that runs on IBM\'s Elastic Storage Server: two POWER servers physically connected to the same disk enclosures. Each serves a recovery group and stands ready to take over the other\'s. Enclosures come in flavors like five-U boxes with eighty four nearline SAS bays for capacity, and two-U boxes with twenty four SSD slots for speed.",')
sub(' "Filesets let you carve', ' "Filesets carve the namespace into subtrees with their own quotas, and independent filesets can have their own inode space. Failure groups tell the system which disks share a fate, a power supply, an enclosure, a rack, so two replicas land in different groups.",')
sub(' "Compare that with A-S-C-I White.', ' "Compare that with A-S-C-I White. Capacity is about eighteen hundred times bigger; bandwidth, about two hundred times. So reading the entire file system once took A-S-C-I White about three hours, and would take Alpine about twenty eight. Capacity has outrun bandwidth, a basic fact of HPC storage and a big reason tiers and policies exist. It\'s a rough comparison, since machines, years, and measurements differ, but the direction is the point.",')
open('script.py','w').write(s)
